  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nFor the `html` end tag, [the spec says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-html-element) the following:\r\n\r\n> An [`html`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-html-element) element's [end tag](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#syntax-end-tag) can be omitted if the [`html`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-html-element) element is not immediately followed by a [comment](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#syntax-comments).\r\n\r\nThis seems counter-intuitive but also not further explained:\r\n\r\nWhy must an `html` end tag be there if the last part of a document is a comment? What is the practical difference between `<!doctype html><title>foo</title><!-- bar -->` and `<!doctype html><title>foo</title></html><!-- bar -->`?\r\n\r\nIs there a part of the spec or documentation that explains this (or am I getting hung up on the wording of the spec)?\r\n\r\nPerhaps to demonstrate the point, can this even be checked on conformance? `<!doctype html><title>foo</title><!-- bar -->`, for example, is valid even though the comment is following an omitted `</html>` end tag. (I don’t mean to make this look absurd, it’s just how the spec seems to read.)\r\n\r\nWhat prevents allowing `html` end tag omission in any case?",
